# useRotateImageOptions

Turns the `rotateImage` option of a background wrapper (`true`, `false` or a partial object) into an explicit flag for rotating the image with a two-finger twist. `CropperBackgroundWrapper` uses it to configure `TransformableImage`.

## Signature

```ts
function useRotateImageOptions(
	rotateImage: RotateImageOptions | boolean
): DefinedRotateImageOptions;

interface RotateImageOptions {
	touch?: boolean;
}

interface DefinedRotateImageOptions {
	touch: boolean;
}
```

## Parameters

| Input                | Result              |
| -------------------- | ------------------- |
| `true`               | `{ touch: true }`   |
| `false` or undefined | `{ touch: false }`  |
| `{}`                 | `{ touch: true }`   |
| `{ touch: false }`   | `{ touch: false }`  |

`CropperBackgroundWrapper` defaults `rotateImage` to `false`, so touch rotation is off unless you enable it.

## Return value

A new `DefinedRotateImageOptions` object. Pass `touch` to `TransformableImage`'s `touchRotate`.

## When to call it

Anywhere. It is a pure function with no effects. When its input is a prop, wrap the call in `$derived`:

```ts
let { rotateImage = false }: CropperBackgroundWrapperProps = $props();

const rotateOptions = $derived(useRotateImageOptions(rotateImage));
```

## Example

See the custom background wrapper on the [useMoveImageOptions](/docs/hooks/useMoveImageOptions#example) page, which uses all three option helpers.

To enable touch rotation on a cropper:

```svelte
<Cropper {src} backgroundWrapperProps={{ rotateImage: true }} />
```

## Differences from React

React memoizes the result with `useMemo`. Here it is a plain function, so wrap it in `$derived` when the input can change. Upstream exports this function but has no page for it.
